This is describe the composite characters of the "Extended TRON Code".
(This is currently a DRAFT and may be changed in future; others may write
comments about it if you have something to write, please.)

Composite character groups may be defined; each group is limited to
one plane, and the character codes within a group are designated as
Beginning Codes, Middle Codes, and Ending Codes (only one each; a
single code cannot be more than one of these). Also, each character
code can only belong to up to one group (although a character code
might not belong to any group, in which case it is a Isolated Code).
(A plane can contain any number of groups, including zero.)

A composite character consists of two or more character codes that all
belong to the same group, of which the first is a Beginning Code, the last
is a Ending Code, and the rest are Middle Codes. Nothing else is allowed
between these codes, including control characters and plane shifts. (If it
does not meet this criteria, then it is a invalid composite character
sequence, but is not otherwise invalid.)

Composite character rendering can be enabled or disabled (usually it will
be disabled if Reveal Codes is active or enabled otherwise), and can be
done independently from kerns and ligatures (although, probably you could
not control kerns independently from ligatures; either both are enabled or
both are disabled). When the composite character rendering is enabled, then
a composite character as defined above is considered as a single character,
for moving cursor, deleting, etc; otherwise, they are separate characters.

(Combining characters and stuff like that are not used; European alphabets
will have separate character codes for accented vs non-accented alphabets
andare not considered to be composite characters. However, composite
characters might possibly be useful for Cangjie, Blissymbols, etc. Kerns,
ligatures, text directions, are also separate than composite characters.)


=== Composite character group definition format ===

The following codes are used:

0x00 to 0x1F = Set mode. This is followed by the bytes that specify which
codes belong to the group, and then if not finished yet then there will be
another mode byte before the further data, if any.

0x20, 0x7F, 0xFE, 0xFF = Indicates the end of the composite character group
definition and the beginning of the VM codes; which of these codes are used
is relevant and is described in the below section. If none of these codes
are present, then the VM codes are not included. If the first byte is one
of these four codes, then only the VM codes are included; the definition of
the groups must come from another source.

The mode byte uses the low 2-bits to indicate the mode for the low byte of
the character code, and the next 2-bits to indicate the mode for the high
byte of the character code. They are:

0 = The same as the previous value or range. If the entire low nybble of
the mode byte is 0, then instead it means a linear range.

1 = A single value used until the next mode byte.

2 = Multiple values.

3 = Range.

The bit4 of the mode byte is set if this is the beginning of the next part
of that group, in the order: Beginning, End, Middle.

The data is given first as the high byte for mode 1, and then the low byte
for mode 1. After that, will be the pairs, being the high byte for mode 2
or 3, low byte for mode 2 or 3, high byte for mode 3, low byte for mode 3.

If it is a linear range, then it is given as four bytes per range, where
they are the high and low byte of the beginning and the high and low byte
of the end (both inclusive), according to "linear2hilo" function.

(Example: <07 40 21 3A 13 61 7A 13 41 5A> means that Beginning Codes are
0x4021 to 0x403A, Middle Codes are 0x4041 to 0x405A, and Ending Codes are
0x4061 to 0x407A.)


=== Composite character VM code ===

Rendering composite characters involves executing a VM code with the
sequence of character codes that make up the composite character as input,
and a set of glyph codes with position information as the output.

This VM code is deterministic, harvard, total, and lacks dynamic
allocation. It is isolated from I/O other than what is described above.

The following register classes are available; each register class has four
registers numbered 0 to 3 (e.g. the first general register is called G0).

* G (general) = Unsigned 16-bits. Initially, G0 and G1 are zero, G2 is the
Beginning Code, and G3 is the Ending Code.

* I (index) = Unsigned 8-bits. Initially, I0 is the number of character
codes in this composite character and the others are zero.

* P (program) = Pointer to a VM code instruction. Initially, P0 points to
the beginning of the program and the others are unset. The address of the
instruction to be executed is P0, and is automatically advanced.

* X (parallel metric) = Position in the direction of writing (right if it
is left-to-right writing; note that it can also be negative). The initial
values are all set to the origin position.

* Y (perpendicular metric) = Position in the direction perpendicular to the
direction of writing (vertical for horizontal writing). The initial values
are all set to the origin position.

There are also classes which are not true registers, but instead use the I
registers to index into the data, and use the same numbers as the I class
(e.g. C1 for the character code indexed by I1).

* C (character codes) = Accesses the input character codes; the first item
is index zero and is the Beginning Code, and the last is the Ending Code.

* CR = Same as C but zero is the last item and goes backward.

* D (data) = Accesses the constant data of the VM code, described below.
(If there is no constant data, then this is not valid.)

* DR = Same as D but zero is the last item and goes backward.

The byte which separates the composite character group definition from the
VM code decides the format of the constant data of the VM code:

* 0x20 means there is no constant data; the first instruction follows.

* 0x7F is not meaningful.

* 0xFE means the next byte is one less than the number of data items, and
then the data items which are 8-bit numbers. (The number of data items can
therefore be 1 to 256.)

* 0xFF is same as 0xFE but each data item is big-endian 16-bits.

All instructions must be complete, including instructions that cannot be
reached, and all jump targets must jump to the beginning of an instruction
and not in the middle of an instruction. (Implementations are not required
to verify this condition, but it is recommended to do so.)

In all cases, if the I1, I2, or I3 register is changed to a greater value
than it previously had, then the corresponding P register becomes unset.

Instruction opcodes 0x00 to 0x3F use the format:

* Bit5-bit4 = Register number.

* Bit3-bit0 = Operation code.

These operations are:

* 0x00 = Exchange P with P0, and exchange I with I0.

* 0x01 = Copy P0 to selected register and then unconditional jump (the jump
offset follows).

* 0x02 = If the value of the I register is zero, then unset the P register
(if register I0 and P0 is selected then stop in this case), otherwise
decrement I and copy P to P0.

* 0x03 = Next byte has the right shift amount in the low nybble and the
left shift amount in the high nybble; affect the G register by first the
right shift and then left shift.

* 0x04 = Reset I to zero.

* 0x05 = Reset G to zero.

* 0x06 = Increment I.

* 0x07 = Increment G.

* 0x08 = Reset X.

* 0x09 = Reset Y.

* 0x0A = A big-endian 16-bit number follows. Do the inverse linear2hilo
function on G, and then subtract the number that was following.

* 0x0B = A big-endian 16-bit number follows. This is added to G and then
the linear2hilo function is made and the result is stored in G.

