Instruction opcodes 0x80 to 0xFF works as follows:

* Bit1-bit0 = Output register number (the output register class is decided
by the specific operation being performed).

* Bit3-bit2 = Input register number.

* bit6-bit4 = Input register type: 0=G, 1=I, 2=C, 3=CR, 4=D, 5=DR. If it is
6 then instead bit3-bit2 is a constant number 0-3, and if 7 then the
bit3-bit2 can be 0 to mean 4, 1 to mean 5, 2 to mean a 8-bit immediate
follows, and 3 to mean a big-endian 16-bit immediate follows.

After that first byte is the immediate value (if applicable) and then the
second opcode byte which works like:

* Bit4-bit0 = Operation code.

* Bit7-bit5 = Additional effect.

The additional effect is one of:

* 0 = None

* 1 = Output the (G,X,Y) triple with the output register number (even if
this operation uses I as the output instead of G). (This is done after the
other operation is done, regardless of the result of the condition.)

* 2 = Output (as 1), and then stop.

* 3 = Output (as 1), and then reset the corresponding X and Y registers to
refer to the origin (zero).

* 4 = Also copy P0 to the P register correspoding to the output register
(after the other operation). (In the case of a jump, the value will be the
