The operation code is one of the below, explained using a C code, where G
and I refers to the output register and (a) means the input operand.

  00   G=a;
  01   G+=a;
  02   G-=a;
  03   G&=a;
  04   G|=a;
  05   G^=a;
  06   G*=a;
  07   G/=a;
  08   G%=a;
  09   G+=a<<8;
  0A   if(G==a)
  0B   if(G!=a)
  0C   if(G<a)
  0D   if(G>=a)
  0E   if(G>a)
  0F   if(G<=a)
  10   I=a;
  11   I+=a;
  12   I-=a;
  13   I&=a;
  14   I|=a;
  15   I^=a;
  16   I=a>>8;
  17   I+=a>>8;
  18   
  19   
  1A   if(I==a)
  1B   if(I!=a)
  1C   if(I<a)
  1D   if(I>=a)
  1E   if(I>a)
  1F   if(I<=a)

For the conditional operations, after the second byte is the jump offset
(see below for the format of the jump offset).

The format of a jump offset is one or two bytes. If one byte, then it is
1 to 255, and is measured in bytes. If two bytes, then the first byte is 0
and the second byte is 0 to 255 and 256 is added, and is measured in bytes.
These are measured from the beginning of the next instruction after this
one, and is always forward. You can jump to immediately after the last
instruction, which stops the program; jumping beyond that is an error.

